Chapter: HostedHttp
  cites Works chapter Http
  cites Works chapter WebRoute
  cites Works chapter HostedServe

 PRISM-10's subject: a hosted target serving HTTP over the host's TCP.

 The subject carries no `.expected` sidecar deliberately, because running the
 subject and reading the output grades nothing: the subject answers a CLIENT.
 The harness is `codex/plugs/elf/hosted-http-arm.ps1`.

 THE ROUTE FUNCTION BELOW IS AN ORDINARY ONE. `route` names no target, calls
 nothing hosted, and would serve unchanged under the bare-metal `web-serve`.
 Proving one route function serves both is the whole of PRISM-10, and a route
 written against the hosted server would prove nothing.

 The port derives from `hosted-kind`, 1 on Linux and 2 on Windows, because WSL2
 forwards a listener inside WSL onto the Windows host and holds the host's port
 after the subject exits. Two targets on one port is measured interference, not
 a theory: `codex/test/hosted-echo.codex` carries the account.

Section: Route

  http-port-base : Integer = 34570
  http-requests : Integer = 2

  route : HttpRequest -> HttpResponse
  route (req) =
    if req.path == "/hello" then http-json "{\"hello\":\"hosted\"}"
    else HttpResponse { status = http-404, content-type = "application/json", body = web-not-found (req.path) }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    let port = http-port-base + hosted-kind
    in act
      fd <- hosted-listen port
      print-line-uni (if fd >= 0 then "listen 0" else "listen " & integer-to-text fd)
      served <- hosted-serve-on route fd http-requests
      print-line-uni ("served " & integer-to-text served)
    end
  end
